{"title":"2000 IMO Problems/Problem 3","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_IMO_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/7/317096889b84df0b72bf69ceb4c82208305f1b0f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n \\ge 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"46\" height=\"16\" /> be a positive integer and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/4/ce4588fd900d02afcbd260bc07f54cce49a7dc4a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lambda$\" width=\"10\" height=\"13\" /> a positive real number. Initially there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> fleas on a horizontal line, not all at the same point. We define a move as choosing two fleas at some points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> to the left of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />, and letting the flea from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> jump over the flea from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> to the point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> so that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/5/a6529a3ae3392f896066e9eebb3cba66640a3bb5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{BC}{AB}=\\lambda$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"70\" height=\"39\" />.\n</p><p>Determine all values of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/4/ce4588fd900d02afcbd260bc07f54cce49a7dc4a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lambda$\" width=\"10\" height=\"13\" /> such that, for any point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" /> on the line and for any initial position of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> fleas, there exists a sequence of moves that will take them all to the position right of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_IMO_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let the fleas initially occupy distinct positions\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/9/c/e9c5395fbab17e118d6433ec43981ae601fd2584.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_1&lt;x_2&lt;\\cdots&lt;x_n.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"156\" height=\"15\" />\n</p><p>Let\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/1/0c10195583d01b859b6108e5d74eb951df43bdc4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D=x_n-x_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"101\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p>be the distance between the leftmost and rightmost fleas, and let \\(d\\) be the smallest positive distance between two consecutive fleas.\n</p><p>Since there are \\(n\\) fleas,\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/0/d/b0d0e962f289c98077bffc4541b3bb764c52e8cf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D\\geq(n-1)d.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"113\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p>Choose the leftmost flea and let it jump over the rightmost flea. If their positions are \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), then the new position \\(C\\) satisfies\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/d/5ed6d57d02aeb41f1492ab65fd95406f7d05a2ab.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC=\\lambda AB=\\lambda D.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"149\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>If\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/2/8/c2815a3fcf4921e9db0927232def13c064d7395e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lambda\\geq\\frac1{n-1},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"88\" height=\"40\" />\n</p><p>then\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/d/ffde627ede70d0492f1b19d8d4407033d1e49190.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC=\\lambda D \\geq\\frac{D}{n-1} \\geq d.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"190\" height=\"41\" />\n</p><p>Thus, the smallest distance between fleas never decreases.\n</p><p>In particular, each time the leftmost flea jumps over the rightmost flea, the rightmost position increases by at least \\(d&gt;0\\). Repeating this process allows the fleas to move arbitrarily far to the right. Hence, for every \\(M\\), all fleas can eventually be placed to the right of \\(M\\).\n</p><p>Therefore,\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/9/77901ebb67e5c0b92b013526545e8b5a5c945d06.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lambda\\geq\\frac1{n-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"83\" height=\"40\" />\n</p><p>is sufficient.\n</p><p>Necessity\n</p><p>Suppose\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/a/02a3064be78c7cb8c0d40228b7edc84ecf917c8b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lambda&lt;\\frac1{n-1}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"88\" height=\"40\" />\n</p><p>Let \\(R\\) denote the position of the rightmost flea and define\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/2/5a22e2cb5927de67f6aa71ffe87dafec94a4e61e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F=R-\\lambda\\sum_{\\substack{\\text{fleas}\\\\\\text{other than the rightmost}}}x_i.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -35px\" width=\"258\" height=\"55\" />\n</p><p>We claim that \\(F\\) never increases after a move.\n</p><p>If the jumping flea does not become the rightmost flea, \\(R\\) remains unchanged while one of the terms in the sum increases, so \\(F\\) decreases.\n</p><p>If the jumping flea becomes the new rightmost flea, suppose it jumps from \\(A\\) over \\(B\\) to \\(C\\). Since\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/6/0e679f6224651322d605e69ebbfa55f6c29faafb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C=B+\\lambda(B-A),$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"158\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p>the increase in the rightmost position is exactly controlled by the corresponding increase in the weighted sum, giving\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/5/4/3548ed6941960faa94891b9a62d26a9b58ad5598.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F_{\\text{new}}\\leq F_{\\text{old}}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"97\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p>Thus \\(F\\) is a non-increasing quantity.\n</p><p>Since every other flea is at most \\(R\\),\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/0/fa0963e3586d43049a20c0afcd5feec3c1bd38fb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sum_{\\substack{\\text{fleas}\\\\\\text{other than the rightmost}}}x_i \\leq(n-1)R.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -35px\" width=\"265\" height=\"55\" />\n</p><p>Hence\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/0/c/b0c384e2f88521895b49e2b4c0640d23501c4779.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F \\geq R-\\lambda(n-1)R =\\bigl(1-(n-1)\\lambda\\bigr)R.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -7px\" width=\"315\" height=\"24\" />\n</p><p>Because\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/5/3e53b09921aa607fabacd447dd1efac2b016d4bc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lambda&lt;\\frac1{n-1},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"88\" height=\"40\" />\n</p><p>we have\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/b/0ebe5d483c790fe75922f1617fc47672260a5172.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1-(n-1)\\lambda&gt;0.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"139\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p>Therefore \\(R\\) is bounded above by a finite constant. Consequently, the fleas cannot be moved arbitrarily far to the right.\n</p><p>Thus,\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/4/6b4d03bc5852abeaee9e784982738581a77e9a4f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lambda&lt;\\frac1{n-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"83\" height=\"40\" />\n</p><p>is impossible.\n</p><p>Therefore, the required values are\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/8/26886ac2d833e8a4406073c145698cb346328abe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\lambda\\geq\\frac1{n-1}}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"99\" height=\"51\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_IMO_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"clear:both; margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_IMO\" title=\"2000 IMO\">2000 IMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_IMO_Problems\" title=\"2000 IMO Problems\">Problems</a></b>) • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c3222\">Resources</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_IMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2000 IMO Problems/Problem 2\">Problem 2</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"34%\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_IMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2000 IMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_IMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2000 IMO Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_IMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2000 IMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_IMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2000 IMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_IMO_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2000 IMO Problems/Problem 6\">6</a>\n</td>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_IMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2000 IMO Problems/Problem 4\">Problem 4</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=IMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"IMO Problems and Solutions\"> All IMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017194028\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.025 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.048 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 190/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 894/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 44/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip re